WEBVTT

00:00:00.015 --> 00:00:02.716
Désirée’s Baby, by Kate Chopin.

00:00:02.716 --> 00:00:08.933
As the day was pleasant, Madame Valmondé drove over to L’Abri to see Désirée and the baby.

00:00:08.933 --> 00:00:11.991
It made her laugh to think of Désirée with a baby.

00:00:11.991 --> 00:00:24.255
Why, it seemed but yesterday that Désirée was little more than a baby herself; when Monsieur in riding through the gateway of Valmondé had found her lying asleep in the shadow of the big stone pillar.

00:00:24.255 --> 00:00:28.783
The little one awoke in his arms and began to cry for “Dada.”

00:00:28.783 --> 00:00:31.443
That was as much as she could do or say.

00:00:31.443 --> 00:00:37.331
Some people thought she might have strayed there of her own accord, for she was of the toddling age.

00:00:37.331 --> 00:00:50.099
The prevailing belief was that she had been purposely left by a party of Texans, whose canvas-covered wagon, late in the day, had crossed the ferry that Coton Maïs kept, just below the plantation.

00:00:50.099 --> 00:01:02.720
In time Madame Valmondé abandoned every speculation but the one that Désirée had been sent to her by a beneficent Providence to be the child of her affection, seeing that she was without child of the flesh.

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:09.229
For the girl grew to be beautiful and gentle, affectionate and sincere,—the idol of Valmondé.

00:01:09.229 --> 00:01:22.095
It was no wonder, when she stood one day against the stone pillar in whose shadow she had lain asleep, eighteen years before, that Armand Aubigny riding by and seeing her there, had fallen in love with her.

00:01:22.095 --> 00:01:27.514
That was the way all the Aubignys fell in love, as if struck by a pistol shot.

00:01:27.514 --> 00:01:36.925
The wonder was that he had not loved her before; for he had known her since his father brought him home from Paris, a boy of eight, after his mother died there.

00:01:36.925 --> 00:01:48.189
The passion that awoke in him that day, when he saw her at the gate, swept along like an avalanche, or like a prairie fire, or like anything that drives headlong over all obstacles.

00:01:48.189 --> 00:01:55.482
Monsieur Valmondé grew practical and wanted things well considered: that is, the girl’s obscure origin.

00:01:55.482 --> 00:01:58.457
Armand looked into her eyes and did not care.

00:01:58.457 --> 00:02:00.997
He was reminded that she was nameless.

00:02:00.997 --> 00:02:06.625
What did it matter about a name when he could give her one of the oldest and proudest in Louisiana?

00:02:06.625 --> 00:02:14.205
He ordered the corbeille from Paris, and contained himself with what patience he could until it arrived; then they were married.

00:02:14.205 --> 00:02:18.936
Madame Valmondé had not seen Désirée and the baby for four weeks.

00:02:18.936 --> 00:02:24.103
When she reached L’Abri she shuddered at the first sight of it, as she always did.

00:02:24.103 --> 00:02:37.786
It was a sad looking place, which for many years had not known the gentle presence of a mistress, old Monsieur Aubigny having married and buried his wife in France, and she having loved her own land too well ever to leave it.

00:02:37.786 --> 00:02:46.179
The roof came down steep and black like a cowl, reaching out beyond the wide galleries that encircled the yellow stuccoed house.

00:02:46.179 --> 00:02:53.557
Big, solemn oaks grew close to it, and their thick-leaved, far-reaching branches shadowed it like a pall.

00:02:53.557 --> 00:03:05.001
Young Aubigny’s rule was a strict one, too, and under it his negroes had forgotten how to be gay, as they had been during the old master’s easy-going and indulgent lifetime.

00:03:05.001 --> 00:03:12.790
The young mother was recovering slowly, and lay full length, in her soft white muslins and laces, upon a couch.

00:03:12.790 --> 00:03:18.670
The baby was beside her, upon her arm, where he had fallen asleep, at her breast.

00:03:18.670 --> 00:03:22.505
The yellow nurse woman sat beside a window fanning herself.

00:03:22.505 --> 00:03:30.199
Madame Valmondé bent her portly figure over Désirée and kissed her, holding her an instant tenderly in her arms.

00:03:30.199 --> 00:03:32.106
Then she turned to the child.

00:03:32.106 --> 00:03:36.560
“This is not the baby!” she exclaimed, in startled tones.

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:40.526
French was the language spoken at Valmondé in those days.

00:03:41.126 --> 00:03:45.833
“I knew you would be astonished,” laughed Désirée, “at the way he has grown.

00:03:45.833 --> 00:03:47.889
The little cochon de lait!

00:03:47.889 --> 00:03:53.355
Look at his legs, mamma, and his hands and finger-nails,—real finger-nails.

00:03:53.355 --> 00:03:55.794
Zandrine had to cut them this morning.

00:03:55.794 --> 00:03:57.835
Isn’t it true, Zandrine?”

00:03:57.835 --> 00:04:02.736
The woman bowed her turbaned head majestically, “Mais si, Madame.”

00:04:02.736 --> 00:04:06.704
“And the way he cries,” went on Désirée, “is deafening.

00:04:06.704 --> 00:04:11.215
Armand heard him the other day as far away as La Blanche’s cabin.”

00:04:11.215 --> 00:04:15.004
Madame Valmondé had never removed her eyes from the child.

00:04:15.004 --> 00:04:19.316
She lifted it and walked with it over to the window that was lightest.

00:04:19.316 --> 00:04:26.964
She scanned the baby narrowly, then looked as searchingly at Zandrine, whose face was turned to gaze across the fields.

00:04:26.964 --> 00:04:34.725
“Yes, the child has grown, has changed,” said Madame Valmondé, slowly, as she replaced it beside its mother.

00:04:34.725 --> 00:04:36.137
“What does Armand say?”

00:04:36.137 --> 00:04:40.898
Désirée’s face became suffused with a glow that was happiness itself.

00:04:40.898 --> 00:04:52.185
“Oh, Armand is the proudest father in the parish, I believe, chiefly because it is a boy, to bear his name; though he says not,—that he would have loved a girl as well.

00:04:52.185 --> 00:04:53.922
But I know it isn’t true.

00:04:53.922 --> 00:04:55.996
I know he says that to please me.

00:04:55.996 --> 00:05:06.418
And mamma,” she added, drawing Madame Valmondé’s head down to her, and speaking in a whisper, “he hasn’t punished one of them—not one of them—since baby is born.

00:05:06.418 --> 00:05:15.103
Even Négrillon, who pretended to have burnt his leg that he might rest from work—he only laughed, and said Négrillon was a great scamp.

00:05:15.103 --> 00:05:18.676
Oh, mamma, I’m so happy; it frightens me.”

00:05:18.676 --> 00:05:20.794
What Désirée said was true.

00:05:20.794 --> 00:05:28.278
Marriage, and later the birth of his son had softened Armand Aubigny’s imperious and exacting nature greatly.

00:05:28.278 --> 00:05:33.717
This was what made the gentle Désirée so happy, for she loved him desperately.

00:05:33.717 --> 00:05:36.547
When he frowned she trembled, but loved him.

00:05:36.547 --> 00:05:40.276
When he smiled, she asked no greater blessing of God.

00:05:40.276 --> 00:05:47.709
But Armand’s dark, handsome face had not often been disfigured by frowns since the day he fell in love with her.

00:05:47.709 --> 00:05:55.866
When the baby was about three months old, Désirée awoke one day to the conviction that there was something in the air menacing her peace.

00:05:55.866 --> 00:05:58.561
It was at first too subtle to grasp.

00:05:58.561 --> 00:06:08.946
It had only been a disquieting suggestion; an air of mystery among the blacks; unexpected visits from far-off neighbors who could hardly account for their coming.

00:06:08.946 --> 00:06:15.252
Then a strange, an awful change in her husband’s manner, which she dared not ask him to explain.

00:06:15.252 --> 00:06:21.446
When he spoke to her, it was with averted eyes, from which the old love-light seemed to have gone out.

00:06:21.446 --> 00:06:28.461
He absented himself from home; and when there, avoided her presence and that of her child, without excuse.

00:06:28.461 --> 00:06:34.578
And the very spirit of Satan seemed suddenly to take hold of him in his dealings with the slaves.

00:06:34.578 --> 00:06:37.307
Désirée was miserable enough to die.

00:06:37.922 --> 00:06:48.822
She sat in her room, one hot afternoon, in her peignoir, listlessly drawing through her fingers the strands of her long, silky brown hair that hung about her shoulders.

00:06:48.822 --> 00:06:58.433
The baby, half naked, lay asleep upon her own great mahogany bed, that was like a sumptuous throne, with its satin-lined half-canopy.

00:06:58.433 --> 00:07:07.316
One of La Blanche’s little quadroon boys—half naked too—stood fanning the child slowly with a fan of peacock feathers.

00:07:07.316 --> 00:07:16.929
Désirée’s eyes had been fixed absently and sadly upon the baby, while she was striving to penetrate the threatening mist that she felt closing about her.

00:07:16.929 --> 00:07:22.753
She looked from her child to the boy who stood beside him, and back again; over and over.

00:07:22.753 --> 00:07:23.342
“Ah!”

00:07:23.342 --> 00:07:28.487
It was a cry that she could not help; which she was not conscious of having uttered.

00:07:28.487 --> 00:07:34.324
The blood turned like ice in her veins, and a clammy moisture gathered upon her face.

00:07:34.324 --> 00:07:39.953
She tried to speak to the little quadroon boy; but no sound would come, at first.

00:07:39.953 --> 00:07:45.405
When he heard his name uttered, he looked up, and his mistress was pointing to the door.

00:07:45.405 --> 00:07:53.610
He laid aside the great, soft fan, and obediently stole away, over the polished floor, on his bare tiptoes.

00:07:53.610 --> 00:07:59.643
She stayed motionless, with gaze riveted upon her child, and her face the picture of fright.

00:07:59.643 --> 00:08:07.700
Presently her husband entered the room, and without noticing her, went to a table and began to search among some papers which covered it.

00:08:07.700 --> 00:08:13.176
“Armand,” she called to him, in a voice which must have stabbed him, if he was human.

00:08:13.176 --> 00:08:14.863
But he did not notice.

00:08:14.863 --> 00:08:16.897
“Armand,” she said again.

00:08:16.897 --> 00:08:19.524
Then she rose and tottered towards him.

00:08:19.524 --> 00:08:24.800
“Armand,” she panted once more, clutching his arm, “look at our child.

00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:26.561
What does it mean? tell me.”

00:08:26.561 --> 00:08:32.794
He coldly but gently loosened her fingers from about his arm and thrust the hand away from him.

00:08:32.794 --> 00:08:36.104
“Tell me what it means!” she cried despairingly.

00:08:36.104 --> 00:08:42.084
“It means,” he answered lightly, “that the child is not white; it means that you are not white.”

00:08:42.084 --> 00:08:48.867
A quick conception of all that this accusation meant for her nerved her with unwonted courage to deny it.

00:08:48.867 --> 00:08:52.480
“It is a lie; it is not true, I am white!

00:08:52.480 --> 00:08:58.645
Look at my hair, it is brown; and my eyes are gray, Armand, you know they are gray.

00:08:58.645 --> 00:09:01.529
And my skin is fair,” seizing his wrist.

00:09:01.529 --> 00:09:07.151
“Look at my hand; whiter than yours, Armand,” she laughed hysterically.

00:09:07.151 --> 00:09:13.013
“As white as La Blanche’s,” he returned cruelly; and went away leaving her alone with their child.

00:09:13.013 --> 00:09:18.700
When she could hold a pen in her hand, she sent a despairing letter to Madame Valmondé.

00:09:18.700 --> 00:09:21.227
“My mother, they tell me I am not white.

00:09:21.227 --> 00:09:23.676
Armand has told me I am not white.

00:09:23.676 --> 00:09:26.441
For God’s sake tell them it is not true.

00:09:26.441 --> 00:09:28.543
You must know it is not true.

00:09:28.543 --> 00:09:29.761
I shall die.

00:09:29.761 --> 00:09:30.958
I must die.

00:09:30.958 --> 00:09:33.633
I cannot be so unhappy, and live.”

00:09:33.633 --> 00:09:35.774
The answer that came was brief:

00:09:35.774 --> 00:09:41.248
“My own Désirée: Come home to Valmondé; back to your mother who loves you.

00:09:41.248 --> 00:09:42.554
Come with your child.”

00:09:42.554 --> 00:09:49.894
When the letter reached Désirée she went with it to her husband’s study, and laid it open upon the desk before which he sat.

00:09:49.894 --> 00:09:55.639
She was like a stone image: silent, white, motionless after she placed it there.

00:09:55.639 --> 00:09:59.373
In silence he ran his cold eyes over the written words.

00:09:59.373 --> 00:10:00.577
He said nothing.

00:10:00.577 --> 00:10:06.069
“Shall I go, Armand?” she asked in tones sharp with agonized suspense.

00:10:06.069 --> 00:10:07.211
“Yes, go.”

00:10:07.211 --> 00:10:08.573
“Do you want me to go?”

00:10:09.173 --> 00:10:11.051
“Yes, I want you to go.”

00:10:11.051 --> 00:10:21.577
He thought Almighty God had dealt cruelly and unjustly with him; and felt, somehow, that he was paying Him back in kind when he stabbed thus into his wife’s soul.

00:10:21.577 --> 00:10:28.307
Moreover he no longer loved her, because of the unconscious injury she had brought upon his home and his name.

00:10:28.307 --> 00:10:35.270
She turned away like one stunned by a blow, and walked slowly towards the door, hoping he would call her back.

00:10:35.270 --> 00:10:37.831
“Good-by, Armand,” she moaned.

00:10:37.831 --> 00:10:39.438
He did not answer her.

00:10:39.438 --> 00:10:41.693
That was his last blow at fate.

00:10:41.693 --> 00:10:44.182
Désirée went in search of her child.

00:10:44.182 --> 00:10:47.487
Zandrine was pacing the sombre gallery with it.

00:10:47.487 --> 00:10:56.482
She took the little one from the nurse’s arms with no word of explanation, and descending the steps, walked away, under the live-oak branches.

00:10:56.482 --> 00:11:00.137
It was an October afternoon; the sun was just sinking.

00:11:00.137 --> 00:11:03.447
Out in the still fields the negroes were picking cotton.

00:11:03.447 --> 00:11:08.753
Désirée had not changed the thin white garment nor the slippers which she wore.

00:11:08.753 --> 00:11:14.406
Her hair was uncovered and the sun’s rays brought a golden gleam from its brown meshes.

00:11:14.406 --> 00:11:20.310
She did not take the broad, beaten road which led to the far-off plantation of Valmondé.

00:11:20.310 --> 00:11:29.381
She walked across a deserted field, where the stubble bruised her tender feet, so delicately shod, and tore her thin gown to shreds.

00:11:29.381 --> 00:11:37.918
She disappeared among the reeds and willows that grew thick along the banks of the deep, sluggish bayou; and she did not come back again.

00:11:37.918 --> 00:11:41.946
Some weeks later there was a curious scene enacted at L’Abri.

00:11:41.946 --> 00:11:46.667
In the centre of the smoothly swept back yard was a great bonfire.

00:11:46.667 --> 00:11:57.502
Armand Aubigny sat in the wide hallway that commanded a view of the spectacle; and it was he who dealt out to a half dozen negroes the material which kept this fire ablaze.

00:11:57.502 --> 00:12:07.446
A graceful cradle of willow, with all its dainty furbishings, was laid upon the pyre, which had already been fed with the richness of a priceless layette.

00:12:07.446 --> 00:12:18.867
Then there were silk gowns, and velvet and satin ones added to these; laces, too, and embroideries; bonnets and gloves; for the corbeille had been of rare quality.

00:12:18.867 --> 00:12:27.964
The last thing to go was a tiny bundle of letters; innocent little scribblings that Désirée had sent to him during the days of their espousal.

00:12:27.964 --> 00:12:32.021
There was the remnant of one back in the drawer from which he took them.

00:12:32.021 --> 00:12:37.352
But it was not Désirée’s; it was part of an old letter from his mother to his father.

00:12:37.352 --> 00:12:38.225
He read it.

00:12:38.225 --> 00:12:41.807
She was thanking God for the blessing of her husband’s love:—

00:12:41.807 --> 00:12:55.187
“But above all,” she wrote, “night and day, I thank the good God for having so arranged our lives that our dear Armand will never know that his mother, who adores him, belongs to the race that is cursed with the brand of slavery.”
